| `turbo/blowoff.ogg` | `sfx100v2_air_03.ogg` | [OpenGameArt - 100 CC0 SFX 2](https://opengameart.org/content/100-cc0-sfx-2) | rubberduck | CC0 1.0 |

CC0 1.0 text: <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode>

## What the source author said

domasx2, on the racing-loop submission:

> These are engine sound loops i made for www.banditracer.eu. Made by cutting out
> a piece from http://www.pdsounds.org/sounds/start_up_car_with_seat_belt_warning_beep
> (public domain), editing to loop semi-smoothly and adjusting pitch. Difference
> between the files is pitch only.

That last sentence is the reason the four engine loops here are renamed by *role*
rather than kept by source name, and it is also a limitation of this pack rather
than a property of the engine voice: **all four are the same recording at four
pitches.** Crossfading between them therefore produces a small change of character
but no new timbre, because there is no second recording to crossfade to. See
"measured properties" below and the note in `Audio/engine_samples.gd`.

rubberduck, on the 100 CC0 SFX 2 pack:

> another pack with 100 CC0 sfx ... Included are: air flowing, door, various
> footstep, glass, hit, item, loops (ambient, machine, construction site,
> highway / street, flowing water), metal / metal hit, misc, stones, switch,
> thunder and wood / wood hit - sounds

`sfx100v2_air_03.ogg` is the pack's "air flowing" sound, 0.285 s. It is used here
as the wastegate voice on a throttle lift. It is a stand-in, not a recording of a
specific turbo, and the file is named for what it is *used for* rather than for
what it was recorded as.

The pack ships three air sounds. Measured in Godot, they are 2.638 s, 1.105 s and
0.285 s; the shortest is the one used, because a lift is a burst and the other two
are a sustained hiss - a turbo that is bleeding off boost for two and a half
seconds sounds like a puncture. `Vehicles/car_db.gd` puts `turbo_blowoff_time` at
0.14..0.20 s across the roster, and 0.285 s is the closest of the three to that.

## Measured properties (not from the filenames)

Measured 2026-10-02 on the files as they sit in this tree, from the PCM itself:

| File | Format | Length | Peak | RMS | Fundamental | Loop seam |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `engine/idle.wav` | 16-bit mono 44.1 kHz WAV | 0.864 s | 0.718 | 0.397 | **43.1 Hz** | 0.012 |
| `engine/cruise.wav` | 16-bit mono 44.1 kHz WAV | 0.667 s | 0.709 | 0.393 | **60.3 Hz** | 0.012 |
| `engine/pull.wav` | 16-bit mono 44.1 kHz WAV | 0.616 s | 0.703 | 0.395 | **70.8 Hz** | 0.007 |
| `engine/redline.wav` | 16-bit mono 44.1 kHz WAV | 0.630 s | 0.709 | 0.395 | **76.7 Hz** | 0.023 |

*Fundamental* is the peak of an autocorrelation over the middle third of the file,
searched 40..400 Hz. *Loop seam* is `|first sample - last sample|` as a fraction of
the file's peak amplitude; anything under about 0.03 is a seam you cannot hear when
the loop is played back to back, which is why these four are used as loops.

**Spectral content:** 99% of the energy of both ends of the ladder is below 689 Hz
(16 x 43.1 Hz), and 90% is below 516 Hz. That is the number that licenses the
`pitch_scale` clamp in `Audio/engine_samples.gd`: playing this material at up to
4x keeps all of it below 2.8 kHz, so nothing aliases and the whole rev range can
be covered by pitch-shifting one recording rather than by a bank.

